      "type": "home-story",
      "settings": {
        "eyebrow": "The Story Behind the Prints",
        "heading": "Her legacy,",
        "heading_em": "carried on.",
        "body": "<p>Kati ran a small textile studio in Montreal — a fashion designer, seamstress, and at one point a baker. She drew on the Modern and Surrealist eras and the decorative line of Art Nouveau, especially Koloman Moser. Some of her designs went out into the world on linens and towels; the most personal she kept for herself. For her granddaughters, she made paper fashion dolls and painted their tiny wardrobes by hand. When she passed, she left behind more than 300 original textile designs, tucked inside her black portfolio case, where they sat in her son's basement for years — waiting.</p><p>Her granddaughter Daisy — a Fashion Institute of Technology graduate who spent her 20s in New York's fashion industry — was chosen to inherit the archive. Now based in the Bay Area with her family, Daisy pairs Kati's prints with modern silhouettes built to last decades, not seasons.</p>",
        "cta_label": "Read the Archive Story",
        "cta_link": "/pages/about"
      },
